## ABSTRACT

This paper presents a rigorous, systems-engineering case for the establishment of a SpaceX Starship launch node on Morocco's Atlantic coast, addressing a documented and quantifiable bottleneck in SpaceX's global launch cadence. Drawing on Federal Aviation Administration environmental impact documentation, peer-reviewed literature on solar-powered direct air capture in the Maghreb, Morocco's aerospace industrial output data, and the legal frameworks governing International Traffic in Arms Regulations (ITAR) exemptions for allied nations, the paper argues that Morocco satisfies the five critical requirements for an overseas Starship site: a clean downrange trajectory, a viable ITAR legal pathway, an established aerospace industrial base, a renewable propellant production architecture that mirrors Mars in-situ resource utilisation (ISRU), and sufficient geopolitical alignment with the United States to support a bilateral Advanced Technology Cooperation Agreement. Detailed engineering corrections are applied to three prior draft vulnerabilities: the trajectory geometry is corrected from an impossible eastward overflight to a southward polar-and-sun-synchronous corridor over open Atlantic; the Sabatier propellant loop is closed by specifying OCP point-source carbon dioxide capture as a near-term supply and solar-powered direct air capture as the long-term Mars-analog architecture; and the offshore propellant delivery system is redesigned from a thermally infeasible 20 km subsea cryogenic pipeline to a mature offshore High Voltage Direct Current (HVDC) power cable architecture feeding an adjacent Propellant Tender Barge. Legal instruments are specified with precision: a Technical Assistance Agreement (TAA) for Phase 0 polar operations and a Manufacturing License Agreement (MLA) for Moroccan Raptor subcomponent production, with an Advanced Technology Cooperation Agreement (ATCA) modelled on the AUKUS Pillar II ITAR exemption framework for Phase I equatorial operations. A phased roadmap, risk register, sonic boom constraint analysis, Western Sahara geopolitical risk mitigation, and a formal negotiated terms framework for Morocco's reciprocal asks are provided in full.

**Keywords:** Starship launch infrastructure; ITAR; Morocco aerospace; Sabatier propellant; direct air capture; offshore launch platform; HVDC barge; ISRU; Western Sahara; multiplanetary civilisation

