## 3. ITAR: THE BINARY GATE

### 3.1 The Regulatory Reality

International Traffic in Arms Regulations classify rockets, including Starship and its Raptor propulsion system, as defence articles under Category IV of the US Munitions List. The transfer of controlled technical data, the provision of defence services, and the operation of ITAR-controlled hardware on foreign soil are each separately regulated and separately licensed. Any overseas Starship facility that does not address this constraint with legal specificity is not a proposal — it is a conceptual sketch. This section provides that specificity.

### 3.2 Phase 0: Technical Assistance Agreement

A Technical Assistance Agreement is a State Department-licensed instrument that authorises US companies to provide defence services and controlled technical data to foreign parties under defined restrictions. Under a TAA, US personnel retain exclusive operational control of all ITAR-controlled hardware. Foreign nationals may perform logistics, fire suppression, perimeter security, and infrastructure maintenance, but are excluded from vehicle access.

This instrument has governed US payload operations at the Guiana Space Centre (Kourou) for decades, establishing clear precedent for a US commercial launch entity operating at a foreign facility under TAA without requiring sovereign enclave status. For Phase 0 polar land-based operations at Dakhla, a TAA is the appropriate instrument. It does not require congressional action, a bilateral treaty, or a White House national security determination. It requires a State Department licence review, which under the existing US-Morocco defence partnership framework is achievable on a 12–18 month timeline.

### 3.3 Phase I: The Advanced Technology Cooperation Agreement and the AUKUS Template

Phase I equatorial operations from an offshore platform, and the full Manufacturing License Agreement for Moroccan Raptor subcomponent production, require a stronger bilateral instrument: an Advanced Technology Cooperation Agreement.

The template for this instrument is the AUKUS Pillar II ITAR framework. The US Congress mandated an ITAR exemption for AUKUS partners through the National Defense Authorization Act for Fiscal Year 2024, granting authority to exempt exports and transfers of defence articles and services to Australia and the United Kingdom from most licensing requirements, provided those countries implement comparable export control systems. [LegalClarity](https://legalclarity.org/aukus-pillar-2-advanced-capabilities-and-legal-reforms/) The State Department published the final AUKUS ITAR exemption rule on December 30, 2025, implementing the framework for licence-free defence trade among the three AUKUS nations. [DLA Piper](https://www.dlapiper.com/en-us/insights/publications/2026/01/department-of-state-publishes-final-rule-finalizing-aukus-defense-trade-itar-exemption)

Morocco is not an AUKUS member. However, the AUKUS precedent demonstrates that Congress can, via an NDAA provision, authorise bilateral ITAR exemptions for strategic allies conditioned on their adoption of comparable export control regimes. The mechanism exists. Morocco's eligibility rests on two conditions: first, that the US-Morocco defence relationship meets the threshold for congressional sponsorship; and second, that Morocco develops an export control framework comparable to US standards. The first condition is addressed in Section 3.4. The second requires Morocco to enact domestic legislation modelled on the Export Administration Regulations, a 24–36 month legislative process that should begin in 2026 irrespective of SpaceX's timeline.

### 3.4 The Diplomatic Foundation for ITAR Progression

The diplomatic scaffolding for an ATCA negotiation was strengthened materially in April 2026. Morocco and the United States signed a defence cooperation roadmap covering 2026 to 2036 at the conclusion of the 14th session of the Morocco-US Defence Consultative Committee, including high-level meetings with US Defence Secretary Pete Hegseth. [HESPRESS](https://en.hespress.com/135917-morocco-us-strengthen-military-ties-with-decade-long-defense-partnership-agreement.html) The roadmap expands cooperation in defence industry development, cybersecurity, advanced technologies, and deeper operational integration. As Under Secretary Elbridge Colby noted at the signing: "This roadmap will guide our historic defence relationship for the next decade, building on a partnership that began 250 years ago when Morocco was the first nation to recognize the United States." [The Hill](https://thehill.com/opinion/international/5899485-historic-moroccan-american-alliance/)

This language — "advanced technologies," "defence industry development" — is substantively different from a generic security cooperation statement. It provides the authorising language under which Morocco can formally propose the ATCA as an annexe instrument to the 2026–2036 roadmap, rather than as a novel and politically unanchored request. Morocco should draft the ATCA text in 2026 and transmit it to the State Department through the existing Defence Consultative Committee channel — not wait for SpaceX to initiate that conversation.

### 3.5 The Enclave Structure: Camp Lemonnier, Not Diego Garcia

The physical governance structure for the launch complex is the Camp Lemonnier model. Morocco cedes operational and legal jurisdiction of a defined 4 km² zone within the Dakhla port perimeter to the United States via a 99-year lease. Inside this zone, US law governs, US personnel control all ITAR-restricted hardware, and SpaceX operates under FAA commercial launch licensing as if on US domestic territory. Outside the perimeter, Morocco retains sovereign title, provides perimeter defence, manages utility supply, operates non-ITAR manufacturing and port logistics, and exercises customs authority over the non-restricted supply chain.

The Camp Lemonnier reference is precise. The US military base in Djibouti operates under a 2017 access agreement providing a defined footprint under US operational jurisdiction on host-nation sovereign territory, with Djibouti providing external security and logistics. The legal precedent is not invented; it is merely unapplied to commercial space. Critically, African Lion 2026, the largest annual US-led military exercise in Africa, brought more than 5,000 troops from over 40 countries across Morocco, including active exercises in Dakhla itself. [The Arab Weekly](https://thearabweekly.com/morocco-us-strengthen-battlefield-coordination-defence-ties) The US military already operates in the precise geography under discussion. The enclave instrument formalises what is already functionally occurring.

**Risk acknowledgement:** State Department interagency processing for an ATCA, even with strong diplomatic foundations, typically requires 3–7 years. This is why Phase 0 operates under a TAA and does not require an enclave. The ATCA is negotiated during Phase 0 operations, not as a prerequisite for them.

