## 4. GEOGRAPHIC AND TRAJECTORY ANALYSIS

### 4.1 Correction of Prior Error

A critical error in prior drafts of this proposal must be stated explicitly before proceeding. An earlier version asserted that Dakhla enjoys "thousands of kilometres of open ocean to the east for a standard eastward orbital insertion." This was factually wrong. The Atlantic Ocean lies to the west of Morocco. An eastward launch from Dakhla would overfly Mauritania, Mali, Niger, and Chad — introducing sovereign airspace violations, populated-area overflight, and regulatory objections that would dwarf any domestic environmental challenge SpaceX currently faces. That trajectory is operationally invalid and has been corrected throughout this version.

### 4.2 The Corrected Trajectory Architecture

Two viable trajectory options emerge from Dakhla's geographic position:

**Option A: Southern Polar and Sun-Synchronous Corridor**

A due-south or south-south-west launch from Dakhla's Atlantic coast places the entire downrange trajectory over open ocean from lift-off to orbit insertion. This trajectory delivers access to polar and sun-synchronous orbits at inclinations between 90° and 100°. The corridor overflies no inhabited land. No sovereign airspace other than Morocco's is traversed. The downrange ocean provides unlimited trajectory abort options. No population-affected debris or aerodynamic hazard areas exist for the first several thousand kilometres of flight.

The commercial value of this corridor is immediate and large. Starlink already operates a dedicated orbital shell at 97.6° inclination specifically for sun-synchronous polar coverage, in addition to primary operational shells at 53°, 70°, and 43°. [Orbital Radar](https://orbitalradar.com/satellites/operator/spacex) Earth observation constellations, signals intelligence satellites, and Arctic broadband infrastructure all require polar and SSO insertion. Dakhla is geometrically ideal for serving the entirety of this market from a ground pad with no offshore infrastructure requirement.

**Option B: Equatorial and Low-Inclination Orbit via Offshore Platform**

For Mars transfer trajectory launches, which require low-inclination departures consistent with Hohmann transfer window geometry, a trajectory correction is required. This is achieved not by adjusting Dakhla's latitude, but by moving the launch point offshore. A launch from 20 km into the Atlantic Exclusive Economic Zone, arcing northward, achieves low-inclination orbit insertion over open ocean. This option requires the offshore infrastructure described in Section 6.

### 4.3 Latitude Comparison

Dakhla sits at approximately 23.7°N. This compares favourably with Cape Canaveral at 28.5°N and Boca Chica at 25.9°N. Launches closer to the equator gain a free velocity contribution from Earth's rotation, reducing fuel needed to reach orbit and increasing effective payload capacity for the same rocket. [Tech Times](https://www.techtimes.com/articles/316637/20260514/spacex-seeks-overseas-starship-launch-sites-break-us-regulatory-cadence-ceiling.htm) While the latitude advantage over existing US sites is not transformative for equatorial missions, it is structurally positive. For polar missions, latitude is not a performance variable — any site above approximately 20°N achieves equivalent SSO insertion efficiency.

