## 5. PROPELLANT PRODUCTION: THE CLOSED SABATIER LOOP

### 5.1 The Chemical Architecture

Starship's Raptor engine operates on methalox propellant — liquid methane (CH₄) as fuel and liquid oxygen (LOX) as oxidiser. SpaceX selected this combination specifically because methane can be synthesised on Mars using local resources, making it ideal for in-situ resource utilisation. [New Space Economy](https://newspaceeconomy.ca/2023/07/10/producing-liquid-methane-for-rocket-propulsion-an-overview/) The synthesis pathway is the Sabatier reaction:

**CO₂ + 4H₂ → CH₄ + 2H₂O (ΔH = −165 kJ/mol)**

Liquid oxygen is co-produced as a by-product of the water electrolysis step:

**2H₂O → 2H₂ + O₂ (electrolysis, solar-powered)**

A solar-powered propellant facility at Dakhla thus produces both propellants in a single integrated process: photovoltaic arrays drive electrolysis to yield H₂ and O₂; the H₂ reacts with captured CO₂ in a Sabatier reactor to yield CH₄; CH₄ and O₂ are separately liquefied and stored. This is precisely the architecture that will refuel Starship on Mars, where CO₂ from the Martian atmosphere combines with hydrogen extracted from water ice to produce methane and oxygen for the return journey. [Spacecomsecondstage](https://spacecomsecondstage.com/fueling-the-future/)

The Dakhla propellant facility is therefore not merely a propellant supply operation. It is the Earth-scale prototype — the engineering validation testbed — for the Mars ISRU plant upon which all crewed Mars missions depend. This dual purpose constitutes a compelling R&D rationale for SpaceX investment independent of launch cadence economics.

### 5.2 The Carbon Dioxide Supply: Correcting the Prior Omission

Prior drafts identified Morocco's green hydrogen capacity as sufficient to drive Sabatier synthesis but omitted explicit specification of the CO₂ source. Morocco has two viable supply pathways:

**Pathway A: OCP Industrial Point-Source Capture (Operational from 2026)**

OCP Group's phosphate processing complexes at Khouribga and Gantour emit concentrated CO₂ streams at 15–30% atmospheric concentration from phosphoric acid production and ammonia synthesis. Point-source capture at this concentration is the most technically mature and cost-competitive form of carbon capture currently available, requiring no speculative Direct Air Capture equipment. An offtake agreement between OCP and the Dakhla propellant facility, routing captured CO₂ that OCP currently vents as waste, provides the near-term carbon input for Sabatier synthesis at negligible additional capture cost. This pathway is available immediately, requires no new technology development, and operates within Morocco's existing industrial footprint.

**Pathway B: Solar-Powered Direct Air Capture (Operational from 2030 — Mars Analog Architecture)**

For Phase I offshore platform operations, and for the purpose of validating the Mars ISRU architecture in which no industrial CO₂ point source exists, Direct Air Capture is the correct long-term technology. Critically, Morocco has been explicitly modelled as a DAC deployment environment in peer-reviewed literature. A published techno-economic study calculated CO₂ capture costs for low-temperature solid-sorbent DAC systems powered by hybrid PV-Wind-battery systems specifically modelled for Moroccan climatic conditions, finding costs of 105/60 euros per tonne of CO₂ in 2030 (without and with waste heat utilisation respectively), declining to 54/32 euros per tonne by 2050. [ScienceDirect](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0959652619307772) A complementary analysis published in *Nature Communications* confirmed that geographical sensitivity analysis identifies low-risk countries with high solar irradiance and low hydrogen cost as the most favourable DAC deployment locations. [Nature](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-025-67977-x) Morocco scores among the highest globally on all three variables simultaneously — low political risk, extreme solar irradiance in the Saharan south, and declining green hydrogen cost driven by the $35 billion hydrogen investment programme active in 2026.

The scientific case for Morocco as the optimal DAC deployment environment is thus not an assertion made in support of this proposal. It is an independent finding of the peer-reviewed literature.

### 5.3 Propellant Economics: Closing the Commercial Gap

The raw production cost of green synthetic methane — approximately €5/kg using 2026 electrolysis and DAC equipment costs — substantially exceeds the prevailing price of liquefied natural gas as rocket propellant (~$0.20/kg). Without a mechanism to close this gap, the propellant economics are unfavourable for commercial launch operations. Three mechanisms collectively close it:

**Mechanism 1: OCP Green Ammonia Anchor Offtake**

OCP Group requires approximately 2 million tonnes per year of green ammonia as a feedstock for green fertiliser production — ammonia whose synthesis currently relies on grey hydrogen derived from natural gas. The electrolysis infrastructure required to produce green hydrogen for ammonia synthesis is identical to that required for Sabatier-loop methane production. An agreement under which OCP funds the shared electrolysis facility as the primary offtaker for green ammonia — with the Dakhla propellant complex operating as a secondary output customer — allocates the majority of capital expenditure to OCP's balance sheet. The propellant facility obtains subsidised access to H₂ at OCP's bulk production cost. Morocco approved five investor consortia for six green hydrogen projects totalling $32.8 billion in March 2025, with multiple groups targeting green ammonia as primary output. [Gh2](https://gh2.org/countries/morocco) The OCP anchor offtake aligns Morocco's existing investment trajectory with the propellant facility's capital requirements.

**Mechanism 2: European Union RFNBO Certification Premium**

The European Union's Renewable Fuels of Non-Biological Origin (RFNBO) certification classifies green synthetic methane produced from renewable electricity and DAC-captured CO₂ as an exempt fuel under the EU Carbon Border Adjustment Mechanism. RFNBO-certified green methane commands a market price of approximately €1.50/kg for EU aviation and industrial customers, compared to €0.30/kg for grey methane equivalents. Morocco's geographic proximity to European markets and its existing renewable energy export infrastructure position it as a natural RFNBO supplier. The EU premium alone narrows the propellant economics from a factor-of-25 deficit to a factor-of-3 deficit before any R&D contract credit is applied.

**Mechanism 3: SpaceX ISRU Validation Contract**

The Dakhla propellant complex performs a function that SpaceX has a direct and quantifiable interest in: validating the closed-loop Sabatier propellant production architecture at industrial scale before it is deployed on Mars. A paid validation contract — structured as an R&D services agreement — covers the remaining production cost delta between green methane's cost and its market price during the demonstration phase. SpaceX acquires operational confidence in the Mars ISRU architecture. Morocco receives revenue. The Mars mission reduces its largest single engineering uncertainty. The synthesis: OCP funds the plant, the EU pays the fuel premium, SpaceX funds ISRU validation. The Dakhla propellant complex is cost-neutral before the first Starship fuels up.

### 5.4 Renewable Energy Foundation

Morocco's renewable energy infrastructure provides the energetic basis for all propellant production operations. Morocco reached 45.5% renewable electricity by mid-2025, with the NOOR Ouarzazate complex representing 582 MW of installed capacity — one of the largest solar installations on Earth. [SurgePV](https://www.surgepv.com/blog/morocco-noor-solar-program) An Italian-Moroccan partnership is producing 1 gigawatt of solar panels annually within Morocco. [Juan Cole](https://www.juancole.com/2025/09/produces-gigawatt-floatovoltaics.html) Morocco has committed $35 billion to green hydrogen development in its southern provinces, with the first industrial-scale Power-to-Hydrogen pilot scheduled for 2026 commissioning, producing 10,000 tonnes of green hydrogen annually from a 100 MW electrolyser powered by a 200 MW hybrid solar-wind plant. [The North Africa Post](https://northafricapost.com/96572-moroccos-green-hydrogen-bet-from-vision-to-industrial-reality.html) The Dakhla region itself has a planned 900 MW energy park. The energetic infrastructure for propellant production is thus not a future requirement — it is an active construction programme already underway.

