## 6. OFFSHORE LAUNCH INFRASTRUCTURE: THE PHOBOS LITE ARCHITECTURE

### 6.1 The Heritage and the Lesson

SpaceX has attempted offshore launch infrastructure before. The Phobos and Deimos oil rigs, purchased in 2021, were intended to serve as floating Starship launch platforms. SpaceX eventually abandoned this project and sold the rigs, though Gwynne Shotwell, SpaceX's President and COO, stated in 2023 that sea-based launches were likely to be part of the company's future. [MIT Technology Review](https://www.technologyreview.com/2025/02/11/1111467/the-dream-of-offshore-rocket-launches-is-finally-blasting-off/) The failure of the Phobos/Deimos programme was not a failure of the offshore concept — it was a failure of the conversion approach. Retrofitting ageing oil infrastructure for cryogenic launch operations proved more complex than anticipated. The correct lesson is not to abandon offshore launch. It is to build purpose-designed infrastructure, not to retrofit legacy equipment.

### 6.2 Platform Specifications

The Phobos Lite architecture is defined by three design principles: purpose-built rather than converted, uncrewed rather than crewed, and thermally decoupled from propellant delivery.

The platform is a purpose-designed jack-up barge, 200 m × 80 m in deck area, constructed on the structural template of offshore wind-turbine installation vessels — a class of marine structures that is mass-produced in South Korean and UAE shipyards with mature engineering standards, established supply chains, and known construction timelines. The platform is stationed 20 km offshore at Dakhla in approximately 60 m water depth, which the local bathymetry supports at that distance from shore. The platform is fully uncrewed during launch operations, with all personnel evacuated by tender vessel prior to ignition. Build time is approximately 24 months in an existing shipyard. Estimated construction cost: $400M, compared to the $2B+ that a converted oil rig conversion would require.

### 6.3 The Cryogenic Thermal Trap: Correcting the Prior Engineering Error

A previous draft of this paper proposed transmitting liquid methane (-161.5°C) and liquid oxygen (-183°C) through a 20 km subsea cryogenic pipeline from shore to the platform. This was a critical engineering error. Pumping cryogenic fluids over 20 km under ocean pressure requires vacuum-jacketed insulation of prohibitive thickness; thermal contraction and expansion cycles across that distance would impose continuous mechanical stress on vacuum-jacketed seals; boil-off losses over 20 km at oceanic ambient temperatures would be economically and operationally unacceptable; and pipeline failure in cryogenic service at depth presents extreme hazard with no precedent in marine engineering. No such pipeline has ever been built because the thermal physics do not permit it at this scale.

### 6.4 The Corrected Architecture: HVDC Power Cable and Propellant Tender Barge

The correct solution transmits electrons rather than cryogenic fluids.

A subsea High Voltage Direct Current (HVDC) power cable — the standard transmission technology for offshore wind farms, with mature design codes, manufacturing capability, and installation vessel fleets — runs from Morocco's onshore solar and wind arrays 20 km to the Phobos Lite platform and an adjacent Propellant Tender Barge. This cable transmits the electrical power generated onshore to drive all offshore operations. HVDC cables at this distance and power scale are commercially available from Prysmian, Nexans, and NKT, with installation vessels operating worldwide.

The Propellant Tender Barge is a separate, non-launching vessel moored immediately adjacent to the launch jack-up. It hosts all phase-change operations: seawater desalination, hydrogen electrolysis, the Sabatier methane synthesis reactor, and cryogenic liquefaction of CH₄ and O₂. Liquid propellant is stored in insulated tanks on the Tender Barge and transferred to the launch vehicle via short, insulated loading arms over a distance of metres — not kilometres — immediately prior to launch. This eliminates the 20 km cryogenic transmission problem entirely by moving all thermally sensitive operations offshore and keeping the transmission medium as electricity.

This architecture resolves four constraints simultaneously: it eliminates all subsea cryogenic hazard; it keeps the launch jack-up structurally clean of large propellant inventories, radically simplifying its blast risk classification; it exploits the most mature offshore power transmission technology available; and it keeps the ITAR boundary clean — the onshore Dakhla enclave produces renewable electrical power; the Tender Barge, which is physically separated from the mainland, performs propellant chemistry and storage outside the primary security perimeter. A secondary physical barrier is maintained between the Tender Barge and the launch platform during fuelling operations.

As an alternative to the Tender Barge, automated uncrewed cryogenic shuttle barges — insulated tank vessels on an automated 20 km round-trip route between a shore-based liquefaction facility and the offshore platform — could serve the propellant delivery function at lower capital cost and with greater operational flexibility. The shuttle barge model accepts slightly higher boil-off losses over the transit but avoids the complexity of offshore liquefaction equipment maintenance. Both options are viable; the choice depends on SpaceX's operational preference for offshore vs. onshore maintenance footprint.

