## 8. MARS ANALOG RESEARCH INFRASTRUCTURE

### 8.1 Existing Programme

The case for Morocco as a Mars analog research location is not an assertion made for the purpose of this proposal. It is a finding of over a decade of peer-reviewed scientific activity. The Ibn Battuta Centre, a Morocco-based research institution operating in partnership with European planetary science programmes, has conducted structured Mars analog field campaigns in Morocco's Saharan terrain since the early 2010s. Field research at Ibn Battuta Mars analogue sites has addressed fundamental questions in Mars exploration science: how to identify sampling sites using remote and local imaging and what information can be gained from shallow subsurface exploration in rocky desert terrain comparable to Martian surface geology. [Liebert Pub](https://www.liebertpub.com/doi/10.1089/ast.2017.1676) This research has been published in *Astrobiology*, a peer-reviewed journal of record for planetary habitability science, establishing Morocco's Sahara as a validated scientific Mars analog in the international literature.

### 8.2 Terrain Characteristics

Morocco's Saharan terrain offers several geological features directly relevant to Mars surface operations: arid hamada (rocky desert pavement) comparable to Martian stony plains; dried lacustrine basins (sabkhas) analogous to ancient Martian lake environments investigated by the Perseverance rover; aeolian dune fields exhibiting active transport dynamics similar to Martian sand seas; and evaporite mineral deposits whose geochemistry is relevant to the search for biosignatures in ancient Martian sediments. These are not superficial visual resemblances. They are geological process analogies validated by geomorphological and geochemical analysis in the primary literature.

### 8.3 Strategic Extension: The Dakhla ISRU Analog Zone

The proposed Dakhla propellant complex, if equipped with an adjacent research facility, constitutes a unique scientific resource: an operating closed-loop Sabatier propellant plant in a Martian-analog terrain environment. Researchers can simultaneously study Martian surface processes in the surrounding desert and validate the engineering performance of the ISRU system intended to operate in that environment. No other proposed or operational Mars analog facility on Earth combines terrain fidelity with operating propellant production infrastructure. This dual capability positions Dakhla as the highest-value Mars analog site in the world for applied ISRU research.

