## 10. GEOPOLITICAL RISK ANALYSIS

### 10.1 The Western Sahara Dimension

Dakhla is located in the Moroccan Sahara — a territory whose international sovereign status remains contested under United Nations frameworks, despite US recognition of Moroccan sovereignty in December 2020 and the adoption of UN Security Council Resolution 2797 on October 31, 2025, which affirmed the Moroccan autonomy plan as the primary settlement framework. Any ITAR White House sign-off process for facilities in disputed territory requires additional national security review beyond what would be required for undisputed Moroccan territory. This is not a dealbreaker — it is a timeline variable that must be planned for.

### 10.2 The Algeria Overflight Risk

Algeria has a documented history of using aerospace objections as diplomatic leverage in its ongoing territorial dispute with Morocco. A southward polar launch corridor from Dakhla does not traverse Algerian airspace — it overflies open ocean from the first second of flight. However, Algeria may file ICAO objections to Aircraft Hazard Areas that extend over its territory during ascent or abort scenarios, even if the nominal trajectory is oceanic. This is not hypothetical; the FAA already creates AHAs over sovereign airspace of Bahamas and Turks and Caicos for Boca Chica missions, as documented in FAA Tiered Environmental Assessment filings.

Mitigation strategy: engage ASECNA (the Agency for Aerial Navigation Safety in Africa and Madagascar) for southern corridor safety certification as the competent multinational authority, independent of bilateral Morocco-Algeria politics. Separately, offer Algeria designated scientific payload allocation on Dakhla polar launches. Converting a potential spoiler into a revenue beneficiary is a more durable solution than attempting to litigate airspace rights through ICAO dispute resolution, which is slow and politically exposed.

### 10.3 The Tan-Tan Fallback

In the event that Western Sahara's contested status produces an insurmountable White House sign-off requirement for ITAR licensing in the timeframe of Phase 0, the designated fallback site is Tan-Tan. Located in undisputed Moroccan sovereign territory approximately 400 km north of Dakhla at 28.4°N latitude — essentially equivalent to Cape Canaveral — Tan-Tan has Atlantic coast access, developing port infrastructure under Morocco's green hydrogen investment programme, and an equivalent southward ocean corridor. The latitude penalty relative to Dakhla is negligible for polar missions. The ITAR approval timeline for Tan-Tan is materially shorter because the White House national security review threshold for disputed territory does not apply.

Phase 0 site selection should formally evaluate both Dakhla and Tan-Tan and permit ITAR counsel's determination to govern the final choice. The engineering analysis does not change materially between the two sites.

### 10.4 US-Morocco Alliance Depth

African Lion 2026, the largest annual US-led military exercise on the African continent, brought more than 5,000 troops from over 40 countries across Morocco, with active exercises in Dakhla. [The Arab Weekly](https://thearabweekly.com/morocco-us-strengthen-battlefield-coordination-defence-ties) Morocco ranks 28th globally in arms imports, with the United States supplying 60% of Morocco's arms imports during 2021–2025. [Morocco World News](https://www.moroccoworldnews.com/2026/04/287360/us-morocco-sign-new-defense-cooperation-roadmap/) Morocco was the first nation to recognise the United States as a sovereign state in 1777. The depth of this relationship is not rhetorical — it is operationally demonstrated every year through African Lion exercises, joint counterterrorism intelligence sharing, and arms cooperation agreements that place Morocco in the highest tier of US non-NATO defence partnerships on the African continent.

