## 14. MUTUAL VALUE FRAMEWORK: MOROCCO'S NEGOTIATED TERMS

Every international launch site agreement that has failed to specify the host country's terms has produced a Kourou outcome: a foreign power receives a pad, and the host country receives a cleaning contract and a modest ground services budget. Morocco must enter this negotiation with explicit, technically grounded, commercially reasonable asks that are stated before any SpaceX meeting is requested.

### Ask 1: Raptor Subcomponent Manufacturing via Manufacturing License Agreement

Morocco already possesses advanced turbomachinery manufacturing capability: it is one of five nations globally capable of aircraft engine production, with Pratt & Whitney and Safran active in its industrial corridors. Morocco joined the elite circle of aircraft engine manufacturing nations in 2025, with the Safran complex launching production of up to 350 engines per year by 2028. [Morocco World News](https://www.moroccoworldnews.com/2025/10/263514/morocco-joins-elite-circle-of-aircraft-engine-manufacturing-nations/)

The request is not full Raptor engine assembly — that remains an ITAR barrier that is not appropriate to seek in Phase 0 or Phase I. The request is precision machining of non-fissile turbopump valve housings, structural brackets, and heat exchanger subassemblies under a US State Department Manufacturing License Agreement (MLA). Under ITAR, a TAA authorises defence services and technical data transfer, while an MLA specifically authorises the manufacture of defence articles abroad under US-controlled licensing and quality oversight — the correct and precise instrument for this purpose. This generates 3,000–5,000 direct high-skilled manufacturing jobs in the Casablanca-Nouaceur aerospace corridor, provides SpaceX with an ITAR-compliant, cost-competitive secondary supply chain for high-volume Raptor subcomponents, and creates an industrial alignment of interest in which Morocco has direct economic incentive for Raptor production continuity.

### Ask 2: African Starlink Gateway Station

Polar launches from Dakhla require ground station infrastructure for Starlink polar shell telemetry and data relay. Morocco should receive, as a condition of hosting polar launch operations, designation as the primary African Starlink gateway for the 97.6° SSO shell — providing broadband coverage to Morocco and its West African economic partners under the Atlantic Initiative framework. This is not a gift; it is the natural infrastructure output of operating the polar launch corridor. The gateway station Morocco hosts and partially operates is necessary for SpaceX to use the Dakhla pad. Morocco should formally receive it as a contractual deliverable, not as an incidental courtesy.

### Ask 3: Ibn Battuta Centre ISRU Co-Authorship and Joint Patent Rights

Morocco's Ibn Battuta Centre has accumulated more than a decade of published Mars analog science. The ask is a formal joint research agreement under which SpaceX ISRU engineers co-investigate the Dakhla closed-loop Sabatier propellant production system with Ibn Battuta researchers, producing co-authored publications and jointly held intellectual property on closed-loop ISRU technology. Morocco receives scientific recognition and IP contribution. SpaceX gains a co-investigation structure that positions the Dakhla propellant demonstration as jointly owned intellectual property — with implications for the eventual Mars ISRU patent portfolio, technology licensing, and scientific credibility with NASA and ESA as agency partners for crewed Mars missions. This is simultaneously a soft power instrument and a hard IP instrument, produced at negligible cost relative to the infrastructure investment.

