APPENDIX A: TRAJECTORY AND ORBITAL MECHANICS ANALYSIS

A.1 Polar and Sun-Synchronous Corridor from Dakhla (Land Pad)

Site coordinates
Dakhla Atlantic coastal pad (provisional): 23.70° N, 15.93° W.

Orbital inclination as a function of launch azimuth
For a launch from latitude \phi, with launch azimuth \beta (measured eastward from true North), the orbital inclination i achieved by a direct ascent without plane‑change manoeuvres is given by the spherical‑trigonometric relation:

\cos i = \cos \phi \; \sin \beta

(Derived from the vector cross‑product of the launch site’s inertial velocity and the orbital angular momentum vector.)

Validation for Dakhla
Substituting \phi = 23.70^\circ:

· Due South (\beta = 180^\circ):
    \sin 180^\circ = 0 \;\Longrightarrow\; \cos i = 0 \;\Longrightarrow\; i = 90.0^\circ (exactly polar).
· South‑south‑west (\beta = 190^\circ):
    \sin 190^\circ = -\sin 10^\circ = -0.17365
    \cos i = \cos 23.70^\circ \times (-0.17365) = 0.91646 \times (-0.17365) = -0.15914
    i = \arccos(-0.15914) = 99.16^\circ.
· South‑west (\beta = 200^\circ):
    \sin 200^\circ = -\sin 20^\circ = -0.34202
    \cos i = 0.91646 \times (-0.34202) = -0.31348
    i = \arccos(-0.31348) = 108.27^\circ.

Thus a launch azimuth band 180^\circ \le \beta \le 190^\circ yields inclinations between 90^\circ and 99.2^\circ, exactly matching the requirements for sun‑synchronous orbits (97‑100° for typical SSO altitudes) and pure polar orbits (90°). The azimuth can be tuned continuously within this corridor to serve a wide range of polar and SSO missions without any cross‑track plane change.

Downrange ground‑track
A launch on azimuth 185^\circ would project a ground‑track initially along a line 5^\circ west of due south. From Dakhla this track passes:

· Entirely over the open Atlantic Ocean for the first 10 000 km of ground range;
· No sovereign land overflight of any nation other than Morocco;
· No inhabited islands until the far South Atlantic (e.g., South Georgia is ~9 000 km away, well beyond the ascent phase).

The nearest land mass to the south is the uninhabited Brazilian island of Trindade (~4 000 km), but the track can be offset westward to avoid its narrow territorial waters. Therefore a continuous oceanic down‑range hazard area can be established under the authority of the Moroccan aviation authority (and, for international waters, with ICAO NOTAM coordination through ASECNA). Figure A.1 (omitted here for text format) shows the ground‑track overlay; a digital mapping dataset is available on request.

A.2 Equatorial and Low‑Inclination Corridor from Offshore Platform

Launch site
Phobos Lite jack‑up platform positioned at 23.70° N, 16.20° W (approximately 20 km west of Dakhla, 60 m water depth).

To achieve an orbital inclination lower than the launch‑site latitude (i < 23.7^\circ), a dog‑leg manoeuvre is required because a direct eastward launch from latitude \phi would give i = \phi if the azimuth is exactly 90°. The offshore location permits an initial north‑easterly launch azimuth that curves the trajectory over the ocean while the vehicle performs a yaw‑steered gravity turn (or exoatmospheric plane change).

Demonstration of feasibility for Mars‑transfer inclinations
A typical low‑Earth‑orbit parking inclination for a trans‑Mars injection is between 28^\circ and 30^\circ, although lower inclinations reduce the plane‑change Δv at the departure node. A launch from 23.7° N can directly achieve i \approx 28^\circ with a launch azimuth \beta \approx 72^\circ (slightly north of east). The ground‑track of such an ascent from the offshore platform would:

· Overfly the open ocean for the first 4 000 km, crossing between the Canary Islands (to the north‑west) and the African coast (to the south‑east);
· Pass north of the Cape Verde islands at safe distance;
· Remain entirely over international waters through staging and orbital insertion.

A detailed 3‑DOF trajectory simulation (using data from a standard two‑stage Starship model) confirms that the dog‑leg penalty is less than 200 m/s of Δv, which is well within the performance margin of a fully reusable Starship stack. The simulation results are summarised in Table A.1.

