APPENDIX B: SABATIER PROPELLANT PRODUCTION – MASS & ENERGY BALANCE

B.1 Stoichiometric Foundations

The Sabatier reaction and water electrolysis combine to produce liquid methane and liquid oxygen from CO₂, water, and renewable electricity.

Sabatier:

\text{CO}_2 + 4\text{H}_2 \rightarrow \text{CH}_4 + 2\text{H}_2\text{O} \quad (\Delta H = -165\ \text{kJ/mol})

Water electrolysis:

2\text{H}_2\text{O} \rightarrow 2\text{H}_2 + \text{O}_2 \quad (\text{energy input} \approx 237\ \text{kJ/mol H}_2\text{O at 25°C})

Overall closed‑loop stoichiometry (recycling the water produced by Sabatier):
For each mole of CH₄ produced, the net inputs are 1 mol CO₂ and 2 mol H₂O (the latter yielding 2 mol H₂ via electrolysis, while the Sabatier regenerates 2 mol H₂O, which is fed back to the electrolyser). The net reaction is:

\text{CO}_2 + 2\text{H}_2\text{O} \xrightarrow{\text{electrolysis + Sabatier}} \text{CH}_4 + 2\text{O}_2

Thus:

· 1 kg CH₄ requires:
  · CO₂: 44/16 = 2.75\ \text{kg CO}_2
  · H₂O (net make‑up): 2 \times 18/16 = 2.25\ \text{kg H}_2\text{O}
· Co‑produces:
  · O₂: 2 \times 32/16 = 4.00\ \text{kg O}_2 per kg CH₄

A Raptor engine runs at a mixture ratio (MR) of approximately 3.6 : 1 LOX : LCH₄ by mass. The closed‑loop synthesis naturally produces O₂ at a ratio of 4.0 : 1, so there is a slight excess of oxygen. In practice, the excess O₂ can be used for other industrial processes or vented.

B.2 Propellant Demand for Starship Missions

A nominal full‑stack Starship/Super Heavy flight consumes roughly 4 600 t of methalox propellant, broken down as:

· CH₄: 1 010 t
· LOX: 3 590 t

(Values derived from SpaceX’s published engine counts, thrust levels, and mixture ratios; exact figures vary with mission profile.)

Phase 0 (12 flights/year) – CH₄ demand = 12 120 t/yr; LOX = 43 080 t/yr.
This quantity is too large for the Phase 0 pilot plant; Phase 0 therefore relies on commercial LNG and LOX supply until the propellant complex ramps up. The pilot plant (1 000 t CH₄/yr) serves only as a technology demonstrator.

Phase I (50 flights/year) – CH₄ demand = 50 500 t/yr; the Dakhla plant scales to 50 000 t/yr, providing virtually all methane fuel.

Phase II (100 flights/year) – CH₄ demand = 101 000 t/yr; the plant expands accordingly.

B.3 Required CO₂ Capture and Electrolysis

CO₂ supply:

· For 50 000 t CH₄/yr: need 2.75 \times 50\,000 = 137\,500\ \text{t CO}_2/\text{yr}.
· OCP point‑source streams (15‑30% CO₂) can easily supply this amount; the Khouribga complex alone emits several million tonnes of CO₂ per year.

Direct Air Capture (DAC) sizing for the Mars‑analog plant (Phase II):

· Target CO₂ capture: 140 000 t/yr.
· Using low‑temperature solid‑sorbent DAC with a specific energy consumption of 1 500 kWh/tCO₂ (thermal + electrical equivalent, as per Fasihi et al. 2019 for Moroccan conditions), the continuous electrical power draw is:
  P_{\text{DAC}} = \frac{140\,000\ \text{t/yr} \times 1\,500\ \text{kWh/t}}{8\,760\ \text{h/yr}} \approx 24\ \text{MW}
  This is a modest addition to the overall plant load.

Electrolysis:

· H₂ required: 0.5 kg H₂ per kg CH₄ → 25 000 t H₂/yr.
· Electrolysis energy: state‑of‑the‑art PEM electrolysers consume ~50 kWh per kg H₂ (lower heating value basis). Total electricity: 25\,000\,000\ \text{kg} \times 50\ \text{kWh/kg} = 1.25\ \text{TWh/yr}.
· Average electrical load: \frac{1.25\times10^9\ \text{kWh}}{8\,760\ \text{h}} \approx 143\ \text{MW}.

Total renewable generation requirement for Phase I (50 000 t CH₄/yr):

· Electrolysis: 143 MW
· DAC (if used): 24 MW
· Sabatier reactor, liquefaction, ancillaries: ~50 MW
· Total continuous power ≈ 220 MW.
· With a capacity factor of 25‑30% for a combined solar‑wind plant in Dakhla’s high‑irradiance region, the installed capacity should be around 800‑1 000 MW, which is consistent with the planned 900 MW Dakhla energy park.

B.4 Economic Model – Closing the Cost Gap

Cost of green CH₄ (2026 baseline):

· Electrolysis OPEX (electricity @ $25/MWh from Moroccan solar): 50\ \text{kWh/kg H}_2 \times 0.025 = \$1.25/\text{kg H}_2 → \$0.625/\text{kg CH}_4.
· DAC CO₂ capture (Pathway B) at €105/tCO₂ (~$115/t) → 2.75 \times 0.115 = \$0.316/\text{kg CH}_4.
· Capital recovery, O&M, liquefaction: add ~$1.0/kg.
· Total cost ≈ \$1.94/\text{kg CH}_4.

Market reference: LNG as rocket propellant ~$0.20/kg; EU RFNBO premium green methane trades at ~€1.50/kg (\(\$1.65/\text{kg}\)). Thus the green methane cost is only ~$0.29/kg above the RFNBO market price.

Bridging the residual gap with OCP H₂ offtake and SpaceX ISRU contract:

· OCP’s anchor demand for green ammonia finances the shared electrolysis capital, reducing the allocated H₂ cost to the propellant plant by 30‑40%.
· A SpaceX ISRU validation contract of $50 million/year (for the demonstration period) covers the remaining differential on 50 000 t CH₄: 50\text{M}/50\,000 = \$1.0/\text{kg} additional support, making the operation cash‑positive.

Consequently, with EU RFNBO certification (which the plant can achieve due to renewable electricity and DAC‑derived CO₂) and the OCP partnership, the propellant complex is economically self‑sustaining by Phase I.

