APPENDIX C: OFFSHORE PLATFORM AND HVDC POWER CABLE ENGINEERING

C.1 Phobos Lite Jack‑up Platform

Design concept
A purpose‑built, uncrewed jack‑up barge derived from wind‑turbine installation vessel (WTIV) hull forms. Main particulars:

Parameter Value
Deck dimensions (L×W) 200 m × 80 m
Leg length 110 m (to penetrate seabed)
Operating water depth 60 m
Elevation above LAT 35 m (air gap + wave clearance)
Maximum payload (launch mount + vehicle) ~5 000 t
Construction cost $400 M (benchmarked to similar WTIV newbuilds in 2026)

Launch loads
The platform must withstand the static and dynamic loads of a Starship/Super Heavy lift‑off. The thrust of the Super Heavy booster (~7 500 tf at lift‑off) is transmitted to the deck through a launch mount that incorporates a water deluge and sound suppression system. Finite element analysis of the deck structure (not reproduced here) shows that standard offshore steel grades (EH36) with stiffened plate design can accommodate the distributed load with a safety factor of 1.5, provided the mount footprint spreads the force over an area of ≥ 400 m². The jack‑up legs experience negligible additional moment because the centre of thrust is close to the hull’s centroid.

Acoustic and thermal protection
A water curtain system around the launch mount reduces acoustic reflections, and the deck is protected by a layer of sacrificial concrete. All sensitive equipment (tender barge, HVDC termination) is located ≥ 100 m from the launch point and shielded by blast walls.

Uncrewed operation
Prior to fuelling, all personnel are evacuated by a fast crew transfer vessel to a safe stand‑off distance (5 km). Remote monitoring and control are conducted via fibre‑optic link embedded in the HVDC cable. This eliminates human‑rated safety requirements for the platform structure during launch.

C.2 Subsea HVDC Power Cable

Instead of transmitting cryogenic fluids, the offshore complex receives electrical power via a standard HVDC link.

Specifications:

· Length: 20 km (shore‑to‑platform)
· Rated power: 400 MW (sufficient to drive electrolysers, DAC, liquefaction and hotel load on the Tender Barge)
· Voltage: ±320 kV DC (typical for offshore wind export)
· Conductor: copper, XLPE insulation, double‑wire armoured
· Installation: plough‑buried to 2 m depth beyond the surf zone; compliant with DNV‑ST‑0359
· Suppliers: Prysmian, Nexans, NKT all offer turnkey solutions for such ratings.

Thermal advantage
HVDC transmission losses at 400 MW over 20 km are less than 0.5%, compared to the prohibitive boil‑off losses and mechanical stresses of a cryogenic pipeline of the same length. The cable is a mature, insurable marine asset.

C.3 Propellant Tender Barge and Transfer System

Tender barge concept
A non‑propelled, moored barge (120 m × 40 m) housing:

· Seawater desalination unit (reverse osmosis, 500 m³/day)
· PEM electrolyser stacks (200 MWₑ capacity)
· Sabatier reactor (multi‑tube fixed‑bed, nickel catalyst, 50 000 t CH₄/yr)
· Cryogenic liquefaction cold boxes (reverse‑Brayton cycle)
· Insulated storage tanks: 2 × 2 000 m³ CH₄, 2 × 5 000 m³ LOX

Propellant is transferred to the launch platform via insulated, vacuum‑jacketed loading arms spanning a 50‑metre gap, using cryogenic couplings certified for marine motion (e.g., TechnipFMC or similar). The transfer rate is 5 m³/min for LOX and 2 m³/min for CH₄, filling the vehicle’s tanks in under two hours.

Shuttle barge alternative
A simpler, lower‑CAPEX option: cryogenic shuttle barges each carrying 500 t of CH₄ and 1 800 t of LOX shuttle between an on‑shore liquefaction plant and the launch platform. Boil‑off during a 20 km round trip is estimated at 0.5‑1.0% of the cargo, which is economically acceptable. This option eliminates all offshore liquefaction equipment and reduces the HVDC power demand (since electrolysis occurs onshore), but introduces a modest vessel traffic load. Both options are retained for trade‑off analysis.

