APPENDIX D: ITAR LEGAL FRAMEWORK AND ENCLAVE AGREEMENT STRUCTURE

D.1 Key Definitions under ITAR (22 CFR Parts 120‑130)

· Defence Article (22 CFR § 120.31): Any item designated in the United States Munitions List (USML). Category IV includes launch vehicles, rockets, and missiles. Starship/Super Heavy and Raptor engines are USML Category IV items.
· Technical Data (22 CFR § 120.33): Information required for the design, development, production, manufacture, assembly, operation, repair, testing, maintenance or modification of defence articles. This includes blueprints, schematics, and operational software.
· Defence Service (22 CFR § 120.32): The furnishing of assistance (including training) anywhere to foreign persons in connection with defence articles. A US person operating Starship abroad and providing guidance to foreign nationals constitutes a defence service.
· Technical Assistance Agreement (TAA, 22 CFR § 124.1): An agreement between a US person and a foreign person for the performance of defence services or the transfer of technical data. It must be approved by the Directorate of Defense Trade Controls (DDTC) and contains a detailed scope, duration, and list of authorized foreign persons. The TAA does not authorize foreign manufacture or production.
· Manufacturing License Agreement (MLA, 22 CFR § 124.2): Authorizes a foreign person to manufacture a defence article using technical data transferred under the agreement. It requires a comprehensive export control plan and is subject to higher scrutiny. An MLA is the correct instrument for Moroccan production of Raptor subcomponents.
· Export exemption for AUKUS (22 CFR § 126.7, final rule 30 Dec 2025): Provides licence‑free export/transfer of defence articles and services between the US, Australia, and the UK for specific projects, provided the recipient country has comparable export controls. The statutory authority is Section 1351 of the FY2024 NDAA.

D.2 Draft Outline of a US‑Morocco Advanced Technology Cooperation Agreement (ATCA)

Drawing on the AUKUS precedent, an ATCA between the US and Morocco would include:

1. Preamble – Reciting the 2026‑2036 Defence Cooperation Roadmap, the shared interest in space launch capability, and Morocco’s commitment to enacting an export control regime equivalent to the EAR/ITAR.
2. Definitions – Adopt USML and Commerce Control List definitions, with a specific “Eligible Items List” for Starship‑related hardware and technical data.
3. Export Control Reciprocity – Morocco to pass and enforce legislation (the “Moroccan Technology Security Act”) criminalising unauthorised re‑export, providing enforcement cooperation, and establishing a licencing authority equivalent to BIS/DDTC.
4. Scope of Exemptions – Exempt from ITAR licensing requirements: (a) US‑origin Starship hardware operated within the Moroccan Enclave; (b) technical data necessary for launch operations and maintenance; (c) manufacturing know‑how for approved subcomponents under an Annex.
5. Physical Security & Enclave Jurisdiction – Define the Enclave (approx. 4 km²) under US exclusive criminal and civil jurisdiction for ITAR‑controlled activities, leased in perpetuity.
6. Safeguarding & Inspections – Joint compliance team; US right to inspect any facility handling controlled items.
7. Duration & Termination – Indefinite, subject to five‑yearly review.

This draft is a diplomatic starting point; the final text would be negotiated through the Defence Consultative Committee.

D.3 Enclave Legal Structure – Camp Lemonnier Precedent

The Enclave at Dakhla would be modelled on the 2017 U.S.‑Djibouti Access Agreement for Camp Lemonnier:

· Morocco retains residual sovereignty; the US exercises “exclusive jurisdiction” over US personnel and all ITAR‑controlled activities inside the Enclave.
· Local civil law (Moroccan) governs non‑ITAR commercial contracts, labour of Moroccan nationals outside the ITAR zone, and environmental baseline compliance.
· A joint commission resolves disputes.
  This structure has been tested for over two decades in Djibouti and is fully compatible with US constitutional requirements and Moroccan law.

