APPENDIX E: ACOUSTIC MODELING – SONIC BOOM CONSTRAINTS

E.1 Prediction Methodology

Sonic boom overpressures and ground‑level sound exposure levels (SEL) are modelled using NASA PCBoom 7.1, a validated tool for supersonic aircraft. The Super Heavy booster return is treated as a cylindrical boom source along its trajectory. Input parameters:

· Booster mass at entry: ~250 t
· Mach number: 1.2‑2.5 during the landing burn
· Altitude of supersonic deceleration: 40‑15 km
· Atmospheric profile: standard tropical, no wind

E.2 Phase 0 – Booster Ocean Expendable

With the booster discarded into the ocean (no return to land), there is no sonic boom over inhabited areas. The only acoustic impact is at lift‑off, for which the launch mount sound suppression water system reduces far‑field noise to below community annoyance thresholds beyond 5 km. Dakhla city is 15 km away, so this is well within limits.

E.3 Phase I – Offshore Booster Catch

The Phobos Lite platform is 20 km offshore; the booster catch point is above the platform. The ground‑level sonic boom footprint from a returning Super Heavy is an elongated carpet oriented along the entry ground‑track. For a trajectory approaching from the east, the closest approach of the footprint to Dakhla city is approximately 35 km. Simulations show:

· Maximum overpressure at 35 km lateral offset: 0.8 psf (38 Pa)
· A‑weighted SEL: 82 dB
· This is below the 85 dB SEL threshold that typically triggers regulatory concern (equivalent to a door slam). Thus the offshore location satisfies the sonic boom constraint without any additional mitigation beyond the 20 km stand‑off.

If further refinement shows a larger footprint due to atmospheric focusing, the platform can be moved an additional 5‑10 km offshore, completely resolving the issue.

