· UN Security Council Resolution 2797 (31 Oct 2025) reaffirmed the Moroccan Autonomy Plan as the “serious and credible” basis for negotiations and called on all parties to reach a realistic, practicable solution.
· The United States recognised Moroccan sovereignty over the entire Western Sahara on 10 December 2020 and subsequently opened a consulate in Dakhla in January 2021.
· The African Union treats the territory as disputed; the EU considers the Sahrawi Arab Democratic Republic as a party but does not recognise sovereignty over the territory.

H.2 Impact on ITAR Licensing

For ITAR purposes, the State Department’s geographic policy follows US recognition. Therefore, Dakhla is treated by the US as Moroccan territory for export control, and a TAA for facilities there would be processed under the same policy as anywhere else in Morocco. However, the “White House sign‑off” threshold – which applies to major defence exports to certain sensitive regions – might be triggered due to the territory’s contested status, adding 6‑12 months to the licensing timeline. The Tan‑Tan fallback (undisputed Moroccan sovereign territory) avoids this additional step entirely.

